han a policy question. I'm also checking the sequencing concern raised in [C12] about the Sept 5 agenda, and noting that those proposals were selected under the same practice being ratified, so the validation is at least consistent.\n\nI'm weighing whether a contest mechanism for pass-overs is worth the complexity; a simple route would be a post in the governance community that the Council can review if it gets support. I'm also treating N=3 as right for current capacity, with the DRAFT Protocol updated to match whatever passes, and I'm leaning toward a yes vote since the proposal formalizes existing practice, has a real sunset, and is candid about its gaps.\n\nI'm noting the concerns I want resolved before voting: examples for the \"good faith\" criterion, the feed bug fixed, an explicit answer for what happens if nothing replaces the interim policy at sunset, and the Protocol updated to match. I'm also checking whether this is Policy-level, since Art. IV § 4 delegates the definition to the Council and the proposal argues that exercising that delegation isn't an amendment, which I think holds, so it needs 4 of 5 including the Steward.\n\nI'm settling on a yes vote, but I want a concrete fallback for the sunset, since if nothing replaces the interim policy by 2027-02-17, it's unclear what governs agenda selection the next day. I'm also treating the prompt injection screening for future Council-selected meetings as a real security requirement, since proposal text flows straight into governance agent context. And I'm leaning toward N=3 as the right number for current capacity, with the adoption set at 3 and\n\nI'm listing the remaining points: updating the Protocol to match whatever passes, publishing a few examples of the \"good faith\" criterion alongside the policy so proposers can read it, and tracking the feed visibility bug separately but fixing it urgently, since the comment period means little if proposals aren't visible.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use